WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.298
Good morning, everyone.

00:00:04.298 --> 00:00:05.262
That was awesome.

00:00:05.262 --> 00:00:07.672
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:07.672 --> 00:00:08.636
No pressure.

00:00:08.636 --> 00:00:12.010
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:12.741
Anyway.

00:00:12.741 --> 00:00:14.214
My name is Sergio Cantu.

00:00:14.214 --> 00:00:17.651
I'm a second-year physics
PhD student, here at MIT.

00:00:17.651 --> 00:00:20.783
I would like to thank
you for having me here.

00:00:20.783 --> 00:00:24.190
So, let's start!

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:28.060
This year's theme for the MLK
celebration is student power.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:31.920
And to be honest, being selected
for the graduate address

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.090
was a very big surprise to me.

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:37.620
Mostly because up
until last year at MIT

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.330
I went from feeling totally
powerless to realizing

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:42.542
that I have a lot more
power than I realize.

00:00:42.542 --> 00:00:45.450
But the thing I'm
proudest is that I learned

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.153
how to help empower others.

00:00:47.153 --> 00:00:48.778
And I hope that by
the end of our chat,

00:00:48.778 --> 00:00:50.840
you will also
learn that you have

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:52.980
more power than you realized.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:56.060
But, let's go back
to the beginning.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:58.800
When news first got out
in my undergraduate school

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.730
about me going to
MIT, it was-- I

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:03.370
was going to as
part of the Bridge

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:05.930
to PhD program-- faculty
and graduate students alike

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:07.550
kept telling me how
much I will learn

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:11.160
and how I would grow
both academically and

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:12.610
professionally.

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:14.720
It felt like
winning the lottery.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:18.430
After a local news story, people
will approach me on the street

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:20.380
and congratulate me
for making it out.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.040
And to please go
back and save them.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.830
You see, I knew the
sentiment too well.

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:27.329
You would, too, if you were
born in the city of Brownsville,

00:01:27.329 --> 00:01:28.030
Texas.

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:31.190
A small city in Texas on
the border by the sea.

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:33.390
More than 35% of
residents in Brownsville

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:34.980
live under the poverty line.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:36.930
The highest poverty
rate nationwide,

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:40.170
according to the 2014 US census.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:43.200
And it was also the least
educated city in 2015,

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.650
with the lowest percentage
of high school diplomas

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:47.765
and high school degrees.

00:01:47.765 --> 00:01:49.640
While this celebration
was going back at home

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:52.970
of me coming to
MIT, I didn't want

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:55.680
to be defined-- you know, to
have my defining characteristic

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.409
at MIT-- to be my background.

00:01:57.409 --> 00:01:58.950
I was afraid that
my upbringing would

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.350
be used against me if
I didn't know something

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:03.230
in lab or in class.

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:06.365
To my surprise, MIT has
felt like a family to me,

00:02:06.365 --> 00:02:09.820
made of people from all
levels of the institution.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:13.415
Some people like-- I need to
stop and say some people--

00:02:13.415 --> 00:02:15.760
Ed Bertschinger who
opened the world to me,

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.380
and Monica [? Huerta ?]
that I think

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:18.755
is somewhere here,
and a lot more

00:02:18.755 --> 00:02:23.601
people that I
couldn't list today.

00:02:23.601 --> 00:02:25.710
ACME, of course.

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:28.280
Later, you know, when I was
admitted into the PhD program,

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.790
I didn't hesitate in
saying yes to MIT.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:33.429
And I can only hope that I
can say the same type of yes

00:02:33.429 --> 00:02:34.220
when I get married.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.650
But even with all
the support, it

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:43.850
didn't prepare me to last year
when my worst fears came true.

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:47.010
You see, Texas shares a border
with the state of Tamaulipas

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.910
on the Mexican side, one
of the most violent states

00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:51.800
the drug related violence.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:55.080
My family has experienced
this violence firsthand

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.010
while I have been here at MIT.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.510
And like many others,
they have been

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:03.020
forced to leave everything
behind and moved to the US

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:06.080
with the hope that
they can be safe.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:08.930
While growing up in Mexico,
this felt very normal.

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:10.970
It was like business like usual.

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:13.820
But now at MIT, I was
able to feel and see

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:15.470
the with a different lens.

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:17.460
And they gave me a
little bit of objectivism

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.270
to the whole situation.

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:21.670
And it left me feeling
powerless, completely

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:22.480
powerless.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.830
Because I felt like I was
so powerless and scared

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:28.920
that if something were
to happen to them,

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:31.980
I would have to leave everything
that I was doing at MIT to go

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:34.450
back to my family and
pick up the estate,

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:37.050
you know if anything
happened to them.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:40.065
I must confess I followed
the textbook example

00:03:40.065 --> 00:03:42.920
at MIT of what not to do.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:44.130
I kept it to myself.

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:48.330
I allowed myself to wear a mask
around MIT, and even at home,

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:51.482
while my family kept
traveling to Mexico.

00:03:51.482 --> 00:03:53.440
It was impossibly difficult
for me to share it,

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:56.000
even with my own MIT family.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.411
And I started isolating
myself even at work.

00:03:59.411 --> 00:04:01.535
It was difficult because
I felt like by opening up,

00:04:01.535 --> 00:04:04.150
I would just perpetuate
a stereotype of violence

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:05.500
in Mexico.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:07.960
But I couldn't stay quiet and
let people like Donald Trump

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:10.810
characterize Mexicans
and Mexican Americans

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:12.060
as rapists and criminals--

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:21.438
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:21.438 --> 00:04:24.010
Unbeknownst to me,
across the country people

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:26.240
were engaging in a
similar conversation.

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:29.045
A Facebook poll in 2015
showed that the two

00:04:29.045 --> 00:04:33.690
top political issues were both
racial issues and immigration.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:35.510
So I decided to be more active.

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:37.890
I decided to do my part
and bring the conversation

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:39.080
to campus.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:41.279
And get to know the
students around me.

00:04:41.279 --> 00:04:42.820
During in my
conversations, I noticed

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:45.390
that even though MIT has
come a long way in terms

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:47.510
of gender and
diversity inclusion,

00:04:47.510 --> 00:04:49.760
there is still a long way to go.

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:52.090
Domestic underrepresented
minorities at MIT

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:55.860
constitute 14% of the total
graduate student population.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:57.930
However, most
underrepresented minorities

00:04:57.930 --> 00:04:59.090
pursue master's degrees.

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:02.584
And being a PhD student, I
have certainly noticed that.

00:05:02.584 --> 00:05:04.250
Getting to know people
around me allowed

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:07.010
me to connect to other
Mexicans and Mexican Americans,

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:11.044
both undergrad and grad at
MIT, Harvard and other places.

00:05:11.044 --> 00:05:13.210
And I found them extremely
passionate about science,

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:16.630
and just like me, they share my
concern for Mexico's problems

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:20.564
as well as diversity
in academic circles.

00:05:20.564 --> 00:05:22.980
I also learned that some of
the grad students have started

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:26.030
an effort called "Clubes de
Ciencia"-- or "Science Clubs"

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:29.340
in English-- to
confront these issues.

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:30.914
And I joined them
in a heartbeat.

00:05:30.914 --> 00:05:33.110
Although we were very
excited about this,

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:34.930
our priorities
still were our lab--

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:36.980
we're grad students
at the end of the day.

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:39.400
So we needed to be
efficient about this.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:40.810
So how do I do it?

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:43.700
Well, I basically-- we basically
approached it the same way

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:45.860
we approach any problem in lab.

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:47.900
If you want to fix
something in your lab,

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.630
you want to
characterize it first.

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:54.310
And for that you need to know
three facts about drug cartels.

00:05:54.310 --> 00:05:55.990
Number one is the
drug cartels in Mexico

00:05:55.990 --> 00:05:58.930
employ close to half a million
people across the country,

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:02.280
making it the fifth largest
employment industry in Mexico.

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:06.060
Number two is that the average
drug cartel members 18 years,

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:09.520
didn't finish middle school,
and lives in extreme poverty.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:13.760
And number three is that
Mexico loves entrepreneurship.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:16.180
Mexico has the largest number
of self-employed citizens

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:20.730
in the world, it's 25%
for men and 17% for women.

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:22.230
So it comes as no
surprise that when

00:06:22.230 --> 00:06:24.200
you interview the criminals
that got convicted

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:28.342
to drug-related crimes
75% will tell you

00:06:28.342 --> 00:06:30.925
that they have a high interest
in starting their own business.

00:06:30.925 --> 00:06:32.800
Which lets you know that
if there's something

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:35.332
that they hate more than
the law, it's their boss.

00:06:35.332 --> 00:06:38.545
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:38.545 --> 00:06:41.080
And MIT knows a thing or
two about entrepreneurship.

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:44.396
We have seen MIT grads companies
such as Intel, Dropbox,

00:06:44.396 --> 00:06:47.050
and my personal favorite,
video game company

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:50.140
Harmonics-- creators
of Guitar Hero.

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:52.640
How does MIT do this?

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:55.310
By providing the skills,
mentorship, and resources

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:57.400
necessary for students
to achieve their goals.

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:00.570
So that meant that our
goal was very clear.

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:02.800
To empower high school
students in Mexico

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:06.145
before they stray away
into a life of crime.

00:07:06.145 --> 00:07:08.250
We do it by bringing in
scientists and engineers

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:09.740
from all over the
world to Mexico

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:12.544
in the form of
one-week workshops.

00:07:12.544 --> 00:07:14.960
It is in these workshops where
students empower themselves

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:17.792
with knowledge while they
build their own lasers,

00:07:17.792 --> 00:07:20.559
photograph the curvature of the
earth with aerostat balloons

00:07:20.559 --> 00:07:23.100
that they build themselves, and
learn about neuroscience with

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:25.920
worms that they grow
themselves-- that's not that--

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:28.120
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:30.850
Despite that, they
experience and build

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:33.370
things that they only imagine
really opens their world.

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:36.671
For example, we had a
grad student from Caltech.

00:07:36.671 --> 00:07:39.500
He had a very successful
biophysics workshop.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:41.910
And his name is Manuel Razo.

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:44.760
However, what students remember
the most about this experience

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.376
was not the techniques,
it was not the concepts

00:07:47.376 --> 00:07:48.790
that they learn from him.

00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:51.440
But the fact that he learned
exactly those same techniques

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:55.245
and concepts when he
was in our first Club de

00:07:55.245 --> 00:07:57.705
Ciencia two years ago.

00:07:57.705 --> 00:07:59.565
The students loved it.

00:07:59.565 --> 00:08:03.776
And so far we have had
the benefit and privilege

00:08:03.776 --> 00:08:06.790
that we have reached more
than 1,000 students so far,

00:08:06.790 --> 00:08:10.874
in nine different cities, in
three different countries.

00:08:10.874 --> 00:08:13.780
In closing, I want
to remind everyone

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:16.510
that have a lot more
power than you realize.

00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:19.066
And that only together we can
fix the problems of today.

00:08:19.066 --> 00:08:20.784
But you have to do something.

00:08:20.784 --> 00:08:21.990
You have to volunteer.

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:22.880
You have to sign up.

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:23.920
You have to cooperate.

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:28.920
In the words of Martin Luther
King, "If you can't fly,

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:29.420
then run.

00:08:29.420 --> 00:08:30.990
If you can't run, then walk.

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:33.309
If you can't walk, then crawl.

00:08:33.309 --> 00:08:37.100
But whatever you do,
keep moving forward."

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:38.380
Thank you.

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:39.930
[APPLAUSE]

